# Only MINIMAL, without PRINT/HOSTPROGS from the reference.

menuconfig SAMPLES_RUST
	bool "Rust samples"
	depends on RUST
	help
	  You can build sample Rust kernel code here.

	  If unsure, say N.

if SAMPLES_RUST

config SAMPLE_RUST_MINIMAL
	tristate "Minimal"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust minimal module sample.

	  To compile this as a module, choose M here:
	  the module will be called rust_minimal.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_MISCDEV
	tristate "Misc device"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust misc device sample, which proves
	  the `file` / `miscdevice` abstractions: it registers
	  /dev/rust_miscdev backed by a small per-file buffer.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_PLATFORM
	tristate "Platform driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust platform driver sample, which proves
	  the `platform` abstraction: it registers a platform driver and
	  a matching platform device (no device-tree change needed, the
	  bus falls back to matching by name), and the driver's probe
	  path owns /dev/rust_platform.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_HWMON
	tristate "Hardware monitor driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust hardware monitor driver: a
	  platform driver plus misc device that reads hardware already
	  proven accessible on this device (the GIC distributor TYPER,
	  the xo clock rate, a GPIO input, and a regulator) and serves
	  the live state as text on /dev/rust_hwmon. It is the Step 8
	  pivot: the QRNG port (`rust_rng`) cannot run here because its
	  registers fault with an SError even with the clock and bus
	  voted, so this driver reads only the resources the Steps 2-7
	  proof driver verified on-device.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_GPIO_IRQ
	tristate "GPIO IRQ counter driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust GPIO IRQ counter driver (Step 9,
	  option 1): a platform driver plus misc device that requests
	  the camera-focus key's real hardware interrupt line (shared
	  with the C `gpio-keys` driver, which already holds it with
	  `IRQF_SHARED`) and counts every time it fires, serving the
	  count on /dev/rust_gpio_irq. It proves the `irq`
	  abstraction against a real hardware line, not a virtual IRQ.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_I2C
	tristate "I2C probe driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust I2C probe driver (Step 9,
	  option 2): a platform driver plus misc device that reads
	  one register live from the Synaptics touchscreen on I2C bus
	  1 and serves the value on /dev/rust_i2c. It is the first
	  consumer of the new `kernel::i2c` abstraction. The read is
	  read-only and serialised on the bus lock against the C
	  touchscreen driver, so it cannot disturb the touchscreen.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_THERMAL
	tristate "Thermal sensor driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust thermal sensor driver (Step 10,
	  option 1): a platform driver plus misc device that reads
	  the SoC temperature straight from the tsens thermal monitor
	  registers, the same way the C `tsens-v2` driver does, and
	  serves it on /dev/rust_thermal. The read is read-only
	  (`readl` on a status register), so it cannot disturb the C
	  tsens driver; the value can be cross-checked against
	  /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0/temp.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_POWER
	tristate "Power control driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust power control driver (Step 10,
	  option 3): a platform driver plus misc device that lets
	  userspace turn the pm660_l1 regulator on and off through
	  /dev/rust_power (`echo "l1 on"` / `echo "l1 off"`). Safety
	  comes from the regulator core's reference counting and
	  from tracking what this driver turned on: it only ever
	  disables a rail it enabled itself, so it can never turn
	  the rail off while the system still holds a reference.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_RTC
	tristate "PMIC RTC reader driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust PMIC RTC reader (Step 11,
	  option 3): an SPMI driver that binds to the PMIC RTC
	  (`qcom,pm8941-rtc`) and reads the hardware clock through
	  the PMIC regmap, serving the seconds since the epoch on
	  /dev/rust_rtc. It is the first consumer of the new
	  `kernel::regmap` abstraction. The read is read-only, so
	  it cannot disturb the C RTC driver; the value can be
	  cross-checked against `date +%s`.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SAMPLE_RUST_BATTERY
	tristate "PMIC ADC battery reader driver"
	help
	  This option builds the Rust PMIC ADC battery reader
	  (Step 11, option 1): an SPMI driver that binds to the
	  PMIC ADC (`qcom,spmi-adc-rev2`) and reads the battery
	  voltage through the PMIC regmap, serving it on
	  /dev/rust_battery. It is the first consumer of the new
	  `kernel::spmi` abstraction. The conversion follows the
	  C ADC driver's sequence (select the VBAT_SNS channel,
	  enable, request, poll for EOC, read the result) and its
	  scaling. The value can be cross-checked against
	  /sys/class/power_supply/battery/voltage_now.

	  This kernel has no module support (CONFIG_MODULES is not set),
	  so the sample is always built into the kernel when selected.

	  If unsure, say N.

endif # SAMPLES_RUST
